---
import Section from "./Section.astro";

/*
 * This section used to argue by comparison. Each of the three cards carried an
 * aside about a named competitor - two vendors' per-user prices, a quotation
 * from another product's own documentation about its mobile support, and a
 * note about what ARK does not replace.
 *
 * They are gone, and the rule that replaced them is worth keeping: this page
 * says what ARK does and why it was built that way, and it does not sell by
 * standing next to somebody else. Naming a competitor to look better than it
 * reads as anxious rather than confident, it dates the moment they ship the
 * thing you quoted them not having, and it spends the reader's attention on a
 * product that is not ours.
 *
 * The one remaining mention of another system is in card three, where ARK
 * imports from named export formats. That is a capability, not a comparison:
 * it tells a reader whether their current data can come with them.
 */
---

<Section
  id="different"
  tone="muted"
  eyebrow="Design decisions"
  title="Three decisions behind the product."
  lead="Most of what a records system does is settled, and ARK does it the ordinary way. These three are choices. They shape everything else, and all three would be expensive to walk back."
>
  <div class="mt-14 space-y-6">
    <article class="reveal rounded-2xl border border-border bg-card p-7 sm:p-10">
      <div class="grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,7fr)_minmax(0,5fr)] lg:gap-14">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">01</p>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-2xl text-balance">Pricing does not count users.</h3>
          <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
            This sector runs on volunteers, students, weekend keepers and one person who comes in on
            Tuesdays. Software priced by user teaches an organization to share a login, and a shared
            login destroys the one thing a record needs: knowing who wrote it.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
            So ARK charges for the animals you are looking after instead. Add everyone who works
            with them - every volunteer, every student, every vet who visits - and the price does
            not move.
          </p>
        </div>

        <aside class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-muted/70 p-6">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold">What a plan limits</p>
          <dl class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-sm">
            <div>
              <dt class="font-medium">Active animals</dt>
              <dd class="text-muted-foreground">
                How many animals are in your care at once. Released and deceased animals keep their
                records without counting against it.
              </dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt class="font-medium">Storage</dt>
              <dd class="text-muted-foreground">For photos and attached files.</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
          <p class="mt-5 border-t border-border pt-4 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            That is the whole list. A rescue with forty weekend volunteers pays what a rescue with
            four pays.
          </p>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="reveal rounded-2xl border border-border bg-card p-7 sm:p-10">
      <div class="grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,7fr)_minmax(0,5fr)] lg:gap-14">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">02</p>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-2xl text-balance">The phone comes first, not the desk.</h3>
          <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
            You notice things at the enclosure, with one hand free and often with no signal. ARK was
            built for that first and given a desk view afterwards, so the phone is not a
            stripped-down version of the real thing.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
            The desk is where the reading, the trends and the paperwork happen. It is the same
            records either way.
          </p>
        </div>

        <aside class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-muted/70 p-6">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold">What that means in practice</p>
          <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            <li>You can log an animal with no signal, and it uploads itself later.</li>
            <li>
              If you write something up at the end of your shift, it still carries the time you
              actually saw it.
            </li>
            <li>
              The photo is taken there and then, not hunted for in your camera roll afterwards.
            </li>
            <li>Buttons are big enough to hit with one hand, wet, in a hurry.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="reveal rounded-2xl border border-border bg-card p-7 sm:p-10">
      <div class="grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,7fr)_minmax(0,5fr)] lg:gap-14">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">03</p>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-2xl text-balance">
            Moving your records in is free, and you can do it yourself.
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
            The reason facilities stay on a system they have outgrown is almost never the price. It
            is that moving years of records is a job nobody has a spare week for.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
            So it is a spreadsheet upload, not a project. Tell ARK which column is which, look at
            exactly what it is about to create, and only then say yes. There are step-by-step
            instructions for files exported from AnimalCare and ZIMS. Run the same file twice by
            mistake and you will not end up with two of every animal - it recognises the ones it has
            already seen and tells you it skipped them.
          </p>
        </div>

        {
          /* Kept, and reworded off the competitor's name. Saying plainly what
            ARK is not is worth more than another paragraph on what it is: it
            is the fastest way for a reader whose need ARK does not meet to
            find that out, and finding it out here is better for both of us
            than finding it out after a migration. */
        }
        <aside class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-muted/70 p-6">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold">What ARK is not</p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            ARK keeps your own records about your own animals. It does not share what you write with
            other institutions, and it is not a pooled database of the world's species.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
            If contributing to that kind of shared network is a reason you keep records, ARK will
            not do it for you, and it is better to hear that now than after you have moved.
          </p>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</Section>
